Report every type's ObjectFlags, not only an object type's.

    Applies to: third_party/typescript-go, from its own root.
    Apply:      git -C third_party/typescript-go apply ../patches/<this file>
    Then:       (cd third_party/typescript-go && go build -o ../../target/tsgo ./cmd/tsgo)

`tooling/bootstrap/bootstrap.sh` does both, and rebuilds `target/tsgo` whenever
a file in this directory is newer than it. Run bootstrap after pulling a change
here; a stale binary is the one failure mode that is not loud, and
`the_pin_reports_object_flags_for_every_type` is what makes the *source* half
loud if a submodule bump drops this.

WHY

The checker keeps two `any`s and tells them apart by one ObjectFlag:

    checker.go:975  c.anyType  = newIntrinsicType(TypeFlagsAny, "any")
    checker.go:976  c.autoType = newIntrinsicTypeEx(TypeFlagsAny, "any",
                                     ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType)

`autoType` is the *evolving* type of `var x;` and `const xs = []` -- "not yet,
the assignments say what it is" -- where a written `any` means "no trust".
Nothing else separates them: both spell `intrinsicName` "any" and both carry
TypeFlagsAny, `anyType`/`wildcardType`/`blockedStringType` report no flags at
all, and `nonInferrableAnyType` uses ContainsWideningType instead.

`newTypeResponse` assigned `resp.ObjectFlags` *inside* `case
flags&TypeFlagsObject != 0:`, so an intrinsic never reported its flags and the
two arrived at a consumer identical. Measured before this patch: all three of a
program's `any` records came over the wire with `objectFlags 0x0`. Giving them
one representation cost Native TypeScript 243 recorded test262 passes -- an
evolving declaration erased throws away the `string[]` its assignments had
already proven.

`autoArrayType` did show the bit, because an array *is* an object type. The
intrinsic it holds did not, which is why the shape looked half-working.

WHY A FORK PATCH AND NOT A RULE OF OUR OWN

The syntactic stand-in -- no annotation, no initializer or an empty-array one,
not a for-in/for-of head -- needs four clauses and still disagrees with the
checker about once per ~1,500 declarations, because what makes a declaration
evolving is a flow analysis and not a spelling. The checker has already done
that analysis and the answer is one bit wide.

REBASING ONTO A NEWER typescript-go

This hunk is additive: it moves one assignment earlier and deletes the copy it
supersedes. For an object type the value is identical to what the case below
assigned, and for every other type the field was zero, which `omitempty` kept
off the wire -- so no existing consumer sees a change. If `newTypeResponse` has
been restructured, the invariant to restore is the one the test asserts: the
assignment stands above `switch flags := t.Flags(); {`, not inside an arm of it.

UPSTREAM

Worth sending: a msgpack/JSON API that reports ObjectFlags for some types and
not others cannot express the checker's own `anyType`/`autoType` distinction,
and any consumer doing type-directed codegen needs it. Not sent yet.

